  "metadata": {
    "source": "Powercor 2026-27 Pricing (structure) + AER-published TSS 2026-31 SCS Indicative Prices (rates)",
    "source_url": "https://www.aer.gov.au/system/files/2026-05/Powercor%20%E2%80%93%202026%E2%80%9327%20%E2%80%93%20Pricing%20%E2%80%93%207%20May%202026.pdf",
    "effective_from": "2026-07-01",
    "notes": "Excluding GST. Peak 4pm-9pm all days; off_peak covers all other times (includes the structural 'Saver' 11am-4pm window, which the indicative-prices model does not price separately from off-peak for 2026-27 -- no distinct Saver rate was published, so it is not modeled as its own period here rather than guessing a value). Small business ToU peak is 9am-9pm workdays. Residential/export CER tariffs and all demand-charge tariffs (NDD/NDM/NDMO/large/HV/sub-transmission) are NOT seeded -- same reasoning as citipower.json (single-window demand schema can't represent the two-season summer/non-summer demand charge without an unmade modeling decision). Source PDF gives tariff codes/structure but not $ rates -- those live in a separate 'Tariff Summary' attachment Powercor doesn't co-publish; rates instead sourced from AER's regulator-hosted TSS.01 indicative-prices spreadsheet (2026-31 Tariff Structure Statement, Jan2025 proposal), cross-checked against the structural PDF's tariff codes (exact match: D1, DD1, PRSTOU, ND1, NDTOU). 'Indicative' prices, not a final DNSP-published price list -- flagged as a slightly different provenance tier than other seeded DNSPs. Powercor and CitiPower are commonly-owned/co-filed (same tariff structure text verbatim, confirmed by direct comparison) -- data independently sourced and verified per-DNSP, not copied across.",
    "last_verified": "2026-08-03"
  },
